### Issue description

The default for `onDelete` and `onUpdate` are inconsistent between different usages of `@ManyToMany`.

### Expected Behavior

The default for both `onDelete` and `onUpdate` in `ManyToMany` relations is `CASCADE`.

```ts
@Entity('posts')
export class Post {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
    id: string;

    @Column()
    title: string;

    @Column()
    content: string;

    @ManyToMany(() => Category)
    @JoinTable()
    categories: Category[];
}

@Entity('categories')
export class Category {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
    id: string;

    @Column()
    name: string;
}
```

This results in the following migration:

```ts
// Table and index creation...
await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE "posts_categories_categories" ADD CONSTRAINT "FK_f50a96e3d32263cc97588d91d6e" FOREIGN KEY ("postsId") REFERENCES "posts"("id") ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE`);
await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE "posts_categories_categories" ADD CONSTRAINT "FK_bb4ea8658b6d38df2a5f93cd506" FOREIGN KEY ("categoriesId") REFERENCES "categories"("id") ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE`);
```

Notice the `ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE` for both constraints.

Now if I modify the `Category` entity to include the inverse side of the `ManyToMany` relation without adding any relation options, I would expect the same migration:

```ts
@Entity('categories')
export class Category {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
    id: string;

    @Column()
    name: string;

    @ManyToMany(() => Post, (post) => post.categories)
    posts: Post[];
}
```

### Actual Behavior

The introduction of the inverse side causes the following constraints to be generated:

```ts
await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE "posts_categories_categories" ADD CONSTRAINT "FK_f50a96e3d32263cc97588d91d6e" FOREIGN KEY ("postsId") REFERENCES "posts"("id") ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE`);
await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE "posts_categories_categories" ADD CONSTRAINT "FK_bb4ea8658b6d38df2a5f93cd506" FOREIGN KEY ("categoriesId") REFERENCES "categories"("id") ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION`);
```

Notice that this time reference to the category ID has `ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION`. This is confusing because I used `ManyToMany` without any relation options and get different constraints.

To get the expected constraints I need to specifically set values for `onDelete` and `onUpdate` like this:

```ts
@Entity('categories')
export class Category {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid')
  id: string;

  @Column()
  name: string;

  @ManyToMany(() => Post, (post) => post.categories, {
    onDelete: 'CASCADE',
    onUpdate: 'CASCADE',
  })
  posts: Post[];
}
```

