### Issue description

1.1.0 wraps a join and its children in parentheses whenever the join has children. For `LEFT` nested under `LEFT` this is semantically a no-op, but it constrains the PostgreSQL planner's join-order search and can cause large plan regressions.

Same underlying change as #12764, different symptom.

### Expected Behavior

Nest only where flat emission would change results — an `INNER` child under an `OUTER` parent. Where parent and child are both `LEFT`, 1.0.0's flat form is equivalent and leaves the planner free.

### Actual Behavior

Nesting is unconditional whenever `childJoins` is non-empty — `query-builder/SelectQueryBuilder.js`:

```js
buildJoinClause(direction, tableName, alias, condition, childJoins = "") {
    const prefix  = childJoins ? "(" : "";
    const postfix = childJoins ? ")" : "";
    ...
}
```

1.0.0 emitted `joinAttributes` as a flat list; 1.1.0 builds a tree and emits it via `createJoinTreeRecursively()`.

### Steps to reproduce

```ts
@Entity() class Country { @PrimaryColumn() id: number }

@Entity() class Profile {
  @PrimaryColumn() id: number
  @Column() countryId: number
  @ManyToOne(() => Country) @JoinColumn({ name: 'countryId' }) country: Country
}

@Entity() class User {
  @PrimaryColumn() id: number
  @Column() profileId: number
  @ManyToOne(() => Profile) @JoinColumn({ name: 'profileId' }) profile: Profile
}
```

```ts
repo.createQueryBuilder('u')
  .leftJoinAndSelect('u.profile', 'p')
  .leftJoinAndSelect('p.country', 'c')
```

1.0.0:

```sql
FROM "user" "u"
LEFT JOIN "profile" "p" ON "p"."id" = "u"."profileId"
LEFT JOIN "country" "c" ON "c"."id" = "p"."countryId"
```

1.1.0:

```sql
FROM "user" "u"
LEFT JOIN ("profile" "p"
           LEFT JOIN "country" "c" ON "c"."id" = "p"."countryId")
       ON "p"."id" = "u"."profileId"
```

Identical results — only planner freedom differs.

### Impact

PostgreSQL 18.1, a query joining 7 tables off a ~570k-row root with one `LEFT` nested under another, ordered by a left-joined column with `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`. On upgrading 1.0.0 → 1.1.0 every pre-upgrade `queryid` in `pg_stat_statements` stopped accumulating at deploy time and traffic moved to new `queryid`s containing `JOIN (`, averaging ~19s.

### Suggested fix

Gate the parentheses on semantic necessity rather than the presence of children: nest when a child would otherwise alter outer-join semantics, emit flat otherwise.

### My Environment

| Dependency | Version |
| --- | --- |
| operating system | Linux (container) |
| node.js | 24.19.0 |
| typeorm | 1.1.0 |
| PostgreSQL | 18.1 |

### Issue description

A query with `relationLoadStrategy: 'query'` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` when the entity has an eager relation declared inside an embedded, and the find options name some other relation without mentioning the embedded.

### Expected behaviour

The query returns the entities with the eager relation loaded, the same as it does when no find options are passed.

### Actual behaviour

`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'owner')`

### Steps to reproduce

```ts
import 'reflect-metadata';
import { Column, DataSource, Entity, ManyToOne, PrimaryGeneratedColumn } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
class User {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() name: string;
}

@Entity()
class Image {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() url: string;
}

class Meta {
    @ManyToOne(() => User, { eager: true, nullable: true })
    owner: User | null;
}

@Entity()
class Post {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() title: string;
    @ManyToOne(() => Image, { nullable: true }) image: Image | null;
    @Column(() => Meta) meta: Meta;
}

async function main() {
    const dataSource = new DataSource({
        type: 'better-sqlite3',
        database: ':memory:',
        entities: [User, Image, Post],
        synchronize: true,
    });
    await dataSource.initialize();

    const user = await dataSource.getRepository(User).save({ name: 'Ann' });
    const image = await dataSource.getRepository(Image).save({ url: 'a.png' });
    await dataSource.getRepository(Post).save({ title: 'Hello', image, meta: { owner: user } });

    // The find options name `image`, and say nothing about the `meta` embedded which holds the
    // eager `owner` relation.
    const posts = await dataSource.getRepository(Post).find({
        relationLoadStrategy: 'query',
        relations: { image: true },
    });
    console.log('loaded', posts.length, 'post(s), owner =', posts[0].meta.owner?.name);
    await dataSource.destroy();
}

main().catch(e => {
    console.error(`${e.constructor.name}: ${e.message}`);
    process.exit(1);
});
```

On 1.1.0 this prints `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'owner')`. On 0.3.31 it prints `loaded 1 post(s), owner = Ann`.

### Cause

`OrmUtils.deepValue()` walks a dotted property path segment by segment without checking that the previous segment resolved to anything:

```js
static deepValue(obj, path) {
    const segments = path.split(".")
    for (let i = 0, len = segments.length; i < len; i++) {
        obj = obj[segments[i]]
    }
    return obj
}
```

`SelectQueryBuilder` calls it in seven places to narrow `select`, `order` and `relations` down to the part that applies to a single relation. All seven treat a missing path as "nothing specified for this relation". The call that brings the throw into an ordinary query is the one in the query-strategy branch of `executeEntitiesAndRawResults()`, which runs for every eager relation of the queried entity whenever find options are set:

```js
relations: this.findOptions.relations
    ? OrmUtils.deepValue(this.findOptions.relations, relation.propertyPath)
    : undefined,
```

For a relation declared inside an embedded, `relation.propertyPath` is `meta.owner`. Find options of `{ image: true }` have no `meta` key, so the loop reads `owner` of `undefined`.

0.3 ships the same `deepValue`. It does not reach this call with an embedded path, because its query strategy registers only the relations named in the find options and joins eager relations into the main query instead. The call that makes the path reachable arrived with #11326.

### Related

#10821 reports the same `deepValue` walking off the end of a path, reached through the `select` call sites rather than this one. It was closed by the stale-issue sweep in September 2025 without a fix, and the code it describes is unchanged.

### Suggested fix

Return `undefined` as soon as a segment resolves to null or undefined, which is what all seven call sites already expect:

```js
static deepValue(obj, path) {
    let value = obj
    for (const segment of path.split(".")) {
        if (value == null) {
            return undefined
        }
        value = value[segment]
    }
    return value
}
```

### Environment

typeorm 1.1.0, better-sqlite3 12.11.1, Node 24.14.1. Reproduced on 1.1.0, not present on 0.3.31.

### Issue description

When an entity has a **lazy** relation on its owning side, and a join column of that relation is also one of the entity's **primary columns**, every `insert()` and `save()` of that entity starts a `SELECT` of the related table. Nothing awaits that query, and its promise has no rejection handler.  The insert itself succeeds and resolves normally. The extra query keeps running in the background. In our case (SQL Server, integration tests) it often deadlocked with the schema teardown that ran after the test, or failed with `Invalid object name` in a later, unrelated test. The query rejects with nothing attached to handle it, so Node reports an unhandled rejection, and test runners blame whichever test is running at that moment.

### Expected Behavior

insert() and save() do not load lazy relations. Building the entity id map reads column values only, and starts no queries.

### Actual Behavior

`ReturningResultsEntityUpdator.insert()` calls `metadata.getEntityIdMap(entity)` to fill `insertResult.identifiers`. For each primary column, `ColumnMetadata.getEntityValueMap()` checks whether the column belongs to a relation. If it does, it reads `entity[relationMetadata.propertyName]`. For a lazy relation that read runs the getter that `RelationLoader.enableLazyLoad()` installed, which calls `RelationLoader.load()` and starts the query.

`getEntityValueMap()` already tries to avoid this:

```js
/**
 * Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor checks if the relation is lazy, in which case value is a Promise
 * DO NOT use `entity[this.relationMetadata.propertyName] instanceof Promise`, which will invoke property getter and make unwanted DB request
 * refer: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/8676#issuecomment-1049906331
 */
if (this.relationMetadata &&
    !Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(entity, this.relationMetadata.propertyName)?.get &&
    entity[this.relationMetadata.propertyName] && ...
```

But `EntityMetadataBuilder` installs the lazy getter on the **prototype** (`enableLazyLoad(relation, entityMetadata.target.prototype)`). For an entity created with `new Entity()`, `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(entity, …)` returns `undefined`, so the guard passes and the next condition reads the property and runs the getter.

The getter's promise has no rejection handler: `setPromise()` in `RelationLoader.enableLazyLoad()` only calls `value.then(…)`. So when the stray query fails, the failure becomes an unhandled rejection.

### Steps to reproduce

No database server is needed. This uses an in-memory sql.js database.

```bash
npm install typeorm@1.1.2 sql.js@1.13.0 reflect-metadata@0.2.2 tsx@4.20.6 typescript@5.9.3
npx tsx poc.ts
```

`tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
    "compilerOptions": {
        "target": "es2021",
        "module": "commonjs",
        "experimentalDecorators": true,
        "emitDecoratorMetadata": true,
        "strict": true,
        "esModuleInterop": true,
        "skipLibCheck": true
    }
}
```

`poc.ts`:

```ts
import 'reflect-metadata';
import {
    Column,
    DataSource,
    Entity,
    JoinColumn,
    OneToOne,
    PrimaryColumn,
    type Logger
} from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
class Profile {
    @PrimaryColumn('varchar')
    code!: string;

    @Column('varchar', { nullable: true })
    bio?: string;
}

@Entity()
class Account {
    // The primary column is also the join column of the lazy relation below.
    @PrimaryColumn('varchar')
    code!: string;

    @OneToOne(() => Profile, { lazy: true, createForeignKeyConstraints: false })
    @JoinColumn({ name: 'code', referencedColumnName: 'code' })
    profile!: Promise<Profile>;
}

const executedSql: string[] = [];
const logger: Logger = {
    logQuery: (query) => executedSql.push(query),
    logQueryError: () => {},
    logQuerySlow: () => {},
    logSchemaBuild: () => {},
    logMigration: () => {},
    log: () => {}
};

process.on('unhandledRejection', (error) => {
    console.log(`UNHANDLED REJECTION: ${(error as Error).message}`);
});

async function main(): Promise<void> {
    const dataSource = await new DataSource({
        type: 'sqljs',
        entities: [Account, Profile],
        synchronize: true,
        logging: true,
        logger
    }).initialize();

    let lazyLoads = 0;
    const load = dataSource.relationLoader.load.bind(dataSource.relationLoader);
    dataSource.relationLoader.load = ((...args: Parameters<typeof load>) => {
        lazyLoads += 1;
        return load(...args);
    }) as typeof load;

    // Remove the related table so the stray query fails in a visible way. 
    await dataSource.query('DROP TABLE "profile"');
    executedSql.length = 0;

    const account = new Account();
    account.code = 'A-1';
    await dataSource.getRepository(Account).insert(account);
    console.log(`insert() resolved. Relation loads started by insert(): ${lazyLoads}`);
    console.log(`SQL after the insert: ${JSON.stringify(executedSql.slice(1))}`);

    // Give the query that insert() did not wait for the time to fail.
    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100));
    await dataSource.destroy();
}

main();
```

Output, the same on 1.1.2 and 0.3.31:

```
insert() resolved. Relation loads started by insert(): 1
SQL after the insert: ["SELECT \"profile\".\"code\" AS \"profile_code\", \"profile\".\"bio\" AS \"profile_bio\" FROM \"profile\" \"profile\" INNER JOIN \"account\" \"Account\" ON \"Account\".\"code\" = \"profile\".\"code\" WHERE \"Account\".\"code\" IN (?)"]
UNHANDLED REJECTION: no such table: profile
```

Control: with only `lazy: true` removed (and `profile?: Profile`), the output is `Relation loads started by insert(): 0`, there is no extra SQL, and there is no rejection.

The `DROP TABLE` only makes the stray query fail where you can see it. Without it, the extra `SELECT` still runs on every insert, unawaited.

### My Environment

| Dependency | Version |
|---|---|
| Operating System | Windows 11 |
| Node.js version | v22.23.3 |
| Typescript version | 5.9.3 |
| TypeORM version | 1.1.2 (also reproduced on 0.3.31) |

### Additional Context

## Possible fix

In `ColumnMetadata.getEntityValueMap()`, decide whether the relation is lazy from the metadata, not from an own-property descriptor. For example, skip the read when `this.relationMetadata.isLazy` is true, or read the loaded value from the data index (`entity["__" + propertyName + "__"]`), as `RelationMetadata.getEntityValue()` already does for lazy relations. Separately, `setPromise()` in `RelationLoader.enableLazyLoad()` could attach a rejection handler, so a failed lazy load does not become an unhandled rejection.

PR #11941 fixes the same kind of unwanted lazy load in `EntityManager.create()`. This report is about the id-map path that `insert()` and `save()` use.

